{"kind":"request","text":"Codebase technical-debt review (codebase-techdebt-review)\n\nWhy. The code grew through many slices, format generations and reworks. The duplication survey of 19 October measured clones at 0.3% of the code. It found the mass in shipped platform versions, v1 forms, lane generations, the GitHub register, fixtures and helpers. Only K1, K2 and K3 were filed from it. Since then the security remediation, the archive repairs, the kernel moves and the workspace rename landed. This review finds the debt that slows the rest of the plan: the archive milestone, the journey MVP and hosted agents.\n\nScope. Main at the head current when the review starts. The review is read-only.\n\nLenses.\n1. Half-finished transitions: code, settings and documents kept for an old form after its successor landed. Examples: old protocol and platform versions, lane generations, v1 forms, the GitHub register, the creation paths retired on artroom-scope. For each, name who still uses it, and whether its removal changes recorded bytes.\n2. Size and shape: the largest modules and functions, and the changes that routinely touch many files. Examples: the scope object, the archive driver, platform/src/destination.ts (2,888 lines) and cli/src/commands.ts (2,053 lines).\n3. Test cost: the suites with the highest run time for each invariant they protect, overlapping witnesses, and suites that time out or fail without a known cause. Examples: the 4,800-input floor-archive case, and the scale-test send refused as unavailable.\n4. Generated files in source: for example the room page bundle in apps/room/page/assets.ts (862,088 bytes), and the seals. Name how each is rebuilt and checked.\n5. Configuration drift: the eleven wrangler configuration files, their settings, the deploy scripts and the deploy commands in the documents.\n6. Documentation and status drift against main.\n7. Dependencies: unused, duplicate or old packages, and the workspace layout after the rename.\n\nOutput. A note at notes/\u003cdate\u003e-codebase-techdebt-review.md with ranked findings. Each finding gives its evidence (path and line, sizes, counts), what it costs now, the proposed action, its risk, and whether it changes recorded bytes. Then builder requests for the items worth doing, in an order that keeps the plan order.","body":{"conditions":"(1) The review head changes documents only; one checker review at the exact head before landing. (2) Every finding cites its evidence, and every measurement says how it was taken; sums are computed by script. (3) No proposed item changes recorded bytes or the meaning of a recorded entry without its own design note. (4) A test removal names the witness for each acceptance case it touches. (5) Plain English per ISO 24495-1 for a technical audience; no product names other than GitHub; no comparisons. (6) An assert resting on this request lists the filed builder requests and their order.","priority":"high: start this sprint, after the archive deploy now in progress","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}